body:
The machine definition should contain enough information to calculate the physical machine-axis positions:

- AC trunnion/table configuration;
- rotary-axis order and directions;
- A-axis pivot position;
- position of the C axis relative to the A axis;
- rotary zero positions and sign conventions;
- axis travel limits;
- tool-length reference.

CamCraft or the postprocessor would transform each tool-tip position and tool orientation into the final `X Y Z A C` machine coordinates.

For simultaneous cutting moves, the Mach4 post should:

- output no `G43.4`;
- enable `G93` inverse-time mode;
- calculate and output an `F` value on every `G1` block;
- restore `G94` after the simultaneous section;
- refuse posting if the required machine geometry is missing.

Indexed 3+2 positioning and simultaneous 5-axis motion should be treated separately, and existing 3-axis output should remain unchanged.

I can provide the measured geometry, pivot positions and axis conventions of my real machine. I can also test generated programs with the spindle stopped and the tool safely above the workpiece before performing cutting tests.
